<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE STATUS BAR'S RECORD-ARM INDICATOR: IT SEES AN ARM IN A ROOT NOBODY IS
  LOOKING AT, IT NAMES WHERE, AND CLICKING IT DISARMS EVERYTHING AS ONE UNDO
  STEP.

  Asked for by the author beside QBX-257's solo badge: "the same display for at
  least one track is armed would also come handy". It is the same display, and
  this case is solo_indicator_clear_all.qxa's shape one flag over, because the
  two badges share their mechanism (SLaneBadge) and must therefore share their
  gate's shape or the shared part would be gated once and trusted twice.

  WHY AN ARM IS WORTH A BADGE AT ALL. Since QBX-260 the arm is what puts a lane
  in the live set: an armed track with monitoring on has its input open and its
  instrument live. "Which tracks are armed" stopped being a question only the
  Record button cares about, and an armed lane can sit in an arrangement tab
  nobody has open.

  THE MASTER TAB IS ACTIVE THROUGHOUT, which is what makes two of the
  assertions mean anything:
    * armRoots=Arrangement;Drums names the root HOLDING each armed lane. A
      master-rooted read — the mistake the QBX-231/233/243/244/245 sweep keeps
      finding — could not answer "Drums" for a lane the master's tree does not
      contain.
    * armOnScreen=0 says not every armed lane is in the root on screen, which
      is the whole reason the badge names a root at all.

  THE INDICES ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT (master 0, arrangement 1). The disarm
  is a macro of one arm-track per armed lane, each rooted at the root that
  holds it; a macro rooted per TAB would resolve "1" against the master and
  disarm a lane nobody asked about while leaving Drums:1 armed. With equal
  indices a wrong-root write would land on the right flag by accident.

  ONE UNDO STEP. undo count="1" puts BOTH flags back, which only holds if the
  two writes were one composite. The redo after it re-applies the disarm and
  balances the counts, since verify-undo compares undo and redo counts.
  verify-undo itself only checks track counts per root, so it is not the claim
  about what the undo restored — the armList= assertions are.

  WATCHED FAILING, each arm alone. Dropping the macro's per-lane root (so the
  disarm resolves against the active tab) fails eight assertions, four of them
  the post-click ones: Drums:1 stays armed while a master lane nobody asked
  about is written. Walking only the master root in sarm::armedLanesInProject
  fails the count, the list, the roots and the on-screen field.

  NO INPUT IS SET ANYWHERE. An arm needs none: it is a model flag, and what it
  then does to the live set is QBX-260's business, gated by
  monitor_needs_arm.qxa. This case is about the badge.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="arm_indicator_status_bar">
  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <create-arrangement name="Drums"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>

    <!-- The FIRST read ATTACHES both indicators: a scripted run never reaches
         the attach sites, so it has to come before the gestures it follows.
         It also states the resting state — no arm, no badge. -->
    <assert-status-bar contains="armShown=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armLanes=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="disarmAllEnabled=0"/>

    <!-- TWO ARMS AT DIFFERENT INDICES IN DIFFERENT ROOTS. arm-track reads its
         path with parseInto, so a BARE path is MASTER-rooted whatever tab is
         active, and the arrangement lane is named "Drums:1" explicitly. -->
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="Drums:1" armed="1"/>

    <assert-status-bar contains="armShown=1"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armLanes=2"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armList=0 Drums:1|"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armRoots=Arrangement;Drums"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armOnScreen=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="disarmAllEnabled=1"/>
    <!-- ...and what is PAINTED, not just what was counted: two lanes over two
         roots cannot be named one root, so the badge says how many. -->
    <assert-status-bar contains="armText=ARM · 2 lanes"/>

    <!-- The solo badge is untouched by any of it: the two are siblings, not
         the same widget, and a shared base that leaked state between them
         would show up here. -->
    <assert-status-bar contains="soloShown=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="soloLanes=0"/>

    <!-- THE CLICK. The master tab is active and has never been left. -->
    <click-arm-indicator/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armLanes=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armShown=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armList=|"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="disarmAllEnabled=0"/>
    <!-- ...and the badge is dead again, by its own refusal. -->
    <click-arm-indicator expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ONE UNDO RESTORES EXACTLY THE TWO FLAGS THAT WERE SET. Not one of
         them, not a third, and not a lane at the same index in the other
         root: the list is the whole answer. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armLanes=2"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armList=0 Drums:1|"/>
    <assert-status-bar absent="armLanes=3"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armRoots=Arrangement;Drums"/>

    <!-- Redo disarms them again, and balances the counts for verify-undo. -->
    <redo count="1"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armLanes=0"/>
    <assert-status-bar contains="armShown=0"/>
  </actions>

  <verify-undo/>
</SActionScript>
